The cuboid has dimensions \(5\times 2\times 2\), so it contains \(20\) small cubes. Since there are four shapes of five cubes each, the dark shape must contain exactly five cubes.

The dark cubes visible on the cuboid are:
- three adjacent cubes in a straight row along the bottom front edge;
- one cube on the top, set one layer farther back, aligned with one end of that row.

To connect the top cube to the row, the dark shape must also contain the hidden cube directly beneath it. Thus the dark five-cube shape is a chain:
- a straight run of three cubes;
- from one end, one cube goes backward;
- then one cube goes upward.

This is a non-planar bent five-cube shape. Rotating the given choices, only shape E has this form; A, B, C are flat/different, and D has the wrong branching/depth arrangement.

\[
\boxed{\text{E (Shape E)}}
